# LIM — every JVM-visible member of `dev.eryalabs.lim`, and every class above them, as it stands
# at this commit.
#
# One line per public or protected constructor, method and field of every class compiled into the
# package — synthetics, bridges, `componentN`, `copy`, `copy$default`, the Parcelize members and
# `CREATOR` included — rendered
#
#     <modifiers> <owner>.<name>(<erased parameter types>): <return type>
#
# and one line per public or protected class, rendered
#
#     <modifiers> class <fully.qualified.Name>: <superclass>, <interfaces, sorted>
#
# with dotted names rather than the slashed JVM spelling: the line denotes the descriptor, it is
# not written as one. Sorted by the key `ApiBaselineTest` classifies a difference by —
# `class <fqn>` for a class and `<owner>.<name>(<params>)` for a member, so the class lines come
# first and a declaration present on both sides with different modifiers, a different return type
# or a different supertype list is reported *changed*, not as one addition plus one removal.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS. An already-compiled consumer resolves a member by name, descriptor *and*
# modifiers, and resolves its own `instanceof`, its own interface calls and its own field access
# against the class above it. `PublicApiAuditTest`'s SIGNATURES snapshot renders neither the
# modifiers nor the compiler's own `$default` bridges, so two of the breaks that cost a consumer a
# NoSuchMethodError move no line in it: an instance method becoming static, and a default argument
# being dropped — the main descriptor survives that one, while `f$default`, the method every
# already-compiled Kotlin caller of the shorter form actually invokes, quietly does not. It is
# blind to the class above them as well, and so was this file until the class lines existed. Which
# class-level breaks that actually covers is worth stating exactly, since the whole point of two
# snapshots is which one catches what:
#   - Invisible to everything else, and the reason these lines exist. A class going final or
#     abstract, and a supertype disappearing when that supertype declares no member — `Revocation`
#     dropping java.io.Serializable, `SignedStatement$Rotate` ceasing to extend `SignedStatement`.
#     Inherited members are not declared members, so no member line moves, here or in SIGNATURES.
#   - Already visible, but badly. `Entry` dropping `: Parcelable` also deletes `describeContents`,
#     `writeToParcel` and the whole `Entry$Creator` block; `StatementVerdict` ceasing to be an enum
#     also deletes `values`, `valueOf` and `getEntries`. Both files already redden on those, as a
#     scatter of removals. The class line turns that into one *changed* line naming the cause.
# The two snapshots are independent on purpose: a change that moves both is a real API change, a
# change that moves only this one is precisely what SIGNATURES cannot see.
#
# WHAT IT IS NOT. It is not a comparison against the published 1.0.0 artifact. That artifact is
# out of reach from the environment this file is generated in, so what is recorded here is the
# surface *at this commit*: evidence for the version decision, which it settles none of. The three
# binary-compatibility items under "Blocked on human" — `shareEntry`'s `)V` -> `)Z`, `@JvmStatic`
# on `Utils`, and the nullability widening — still need a human.
#
# WHAT IT IS PINNED TO BESIDES THE API. `$default`, `access$` and bridge members are emitted by
# the Kotlin compiler, not written by anyone, so a routine toolchain bump can move lines here with
# no API change at all. Generated with:
#
#     Kotlin 2.0.21, AGP 8.13.2, jvmTarget 11
#
# If a diff here surprises you, check that first. `ApiBaselineTest` asserts all three numbers
# above against `gradle/libs.versions.toml` and `LIM/build.gradle.kts`, so they cannot go stale
# without a failure.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT RENDER, so that nobody reads more into a green run than it says.
#   - Kotlin nullability, and generic type arguments: `Map<String, TypedField>` narrowing to
#     `Map<String, String>` is a source break that changes no descriptor, and SIGNATURES already
#     renders type arguments to catch exactly that. A class line's supertypes are erased for the
#     same reason — `Parceler<Entry>` is recorded as `kotlinx.parcelize.Parceler`.
#   - Kotlin *visibility*, which is the one class-level break still unpinned anywhere. An
#     `internal class` compiles to a public JVM class with no mangling and no access bit, so a
#     published type going `internal` — a source break for every consumer — is invisible to the
#     reflection this file is built on, and SIGNATURES cannot see it either. Closing that needs
#     `@Metadata` parsing or `kotlin-reflect`; adding a dependency is a human's decision, so the
#     gap is recorded rather than filled. What *is* pinned above the members: the JVM modifiers,
#     the `interface`/`enum`/`annotation` kind, and the erased supertype list.
#   - Private and package-private members, which no consumer can link against; package-private
#     classes, for the same reason, though this package has none and that filter excludes nothing
#     today; and anonymous classes, which no consumer can name.
#   - Any future *subpackage*: one package directory is enumerated, exactly as the audit does.
#
# TO UPDATE. Run `./gradlew :LIM:testDebugUnitTest --tests '*ApiBaselineTest'`. On a mismatch the
# test writes a regenerated body to `LIM/build/api/lim.api.actual` and names it in the failure.
# Replace the lines below with that body, keep this header, and read the verdict words before you
# do: *added* is permitted by stop rule 10, *removed* and *changed* are what it forbids.
